#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 420.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162916} :activity/id #uuid "402c5791-a328-536f-b52e-96d65b27ede7" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049815 :topic/id #uuid "15e1a0d1-d9d9-5488-943d-228c2ac29ecd" :topic/math-academy-id 3062} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162916 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163250 :tutorial/content "If $X$ and $Y$ are *independent* random variables, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[X+Y]} = \\textrm{Var}[X]+ \\textrm{Var}[Y] ,\n$$\n\nand\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[X-Y]} = \\textrm{Var}[X] + \\textrm{Var}[Y]  .\n$$\n\n**Watch out!** When computing the variance of a *difference* of independent random variables, we *add* the variances!" :tutorial/id #uuid "3c595d74-9638-52f2-a4af-79dfe2ded69c" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5023 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "85c48a68-bb08-5e36-af92-8eb493ace0d1" :step/math-academy-id 31412 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162994}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162981 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162062 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161862 :question/id #uuid "1df04c71-caae-5a7e-83ac-4dd578b7f766" :question/math-academy-id "e-9122"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "52596d3a-a7af-55a0-97a8-8a6158b65638" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048731}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162275} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162293} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427840} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427842} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427845} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427849} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427850} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444651} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444652} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444653}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "0a0041f4-e6ca-5360-9d1e-8b7a07a7bccd" :step/math-academy-id 33995 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162992}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162992 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162067 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161867 :question/id #uuid "0c3690cd-f90e-5d82-ba7b-09d52de47b80" :question/math-academy-id "e-9143"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0df88138-7309-5a09-9a96-5e26029f0186" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162276} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162277} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427837} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427839} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427846} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427847} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427848} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444650}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating a Combined Variance Given Some Raw Moments"} :step/id #uuid "24790bde-943d-5850-8dad-fcae56637f85" :step/math-academy-id 34081} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162994 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162068 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186161868 :question/id #uuid "70881840-32f1-5bab-947d-00d77f0ff71e" :question/math-academy-id "e-9181"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "0e3dad37-612c-56f1-a571-360fafe336b4" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050835}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162295} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162296} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427838} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427841} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427843} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186427844}] :knowledge-point/title "Calculating the Variance of a Sum or Difference of Independent Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "d8885fb1-6c97-579e-9795-07aef731fb3e" :step/math-academy-id 34246 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163002}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163002 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186163284 :tutorial/content "We have been using the fact that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[X+Y]} = \\textrm{Var}[X]+ \\textrm{Var}[Y]  .\n$$\n\nUsing the property that $\\textrm{Var}[aX] = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X],$ we can show that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}{[aX+bY]} = a^2\\textrm{Var}[X]+ b^2\\textrm{Var}[Y] ,\n$$\n\nwhere $a$ and $b$ are constants.\n\nFor example, for two independent random variables $X$ and $Y$, we have \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[4X+3Y] & ={4}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{3}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & =16\\text{Var}[X]+9\\text{Var}[Y]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nOnce again, we need to show caution when considering a difference. For example,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[4X-3Y] & =\\text{Var}[4X+(-3)Y] \\\\  & ={4}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+(-3)^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & =16\\text{Var}[X]+9\\text{Var}[Y].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nMore generally, if the random variables $X_1, X_2,\\ldots, X_n,$ are *mutually independent*, then\n\n$$\n\\textrm{Var}\\left[\\sum_{i=1}^n a_i X_i\\right] = \\sum_{i=1}^n a_i^2\\textrm{Var}[X_i].\n$$" :tutorial/id #uuid "d56df0e7-9547-5cad-8697-f7c9d96ff12a" :tutorial/math-academy-id 5514 :tutorial/title "The Variance of a Sum of Scaled Random Variables"} :step/id #uuid "bcaba5d6-1140-5eb4-b9e1-ea74caf408c7" :step/math-academy-id 34354 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162981}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sums of Independent Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :activity.type/lesson} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186162196}] [{:activity/course {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186046658} :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444658} :activity/id #uuid "2dccda47-5766-55d3-9dca-841c1a2a389a" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049815 :topic/id #uuid "15e1a0d1-d9d9-5488-943d-228c2ac29ecd" :topic/math-academy-id 3062} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444654 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444653 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "61bb06c0-eefd-5dd5-9a60-e0cb63bc7e4a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "112" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444632} {:answer/id #uuid "62e4e3c6-27ff-5891-9e8d-1d5f6c4b8e3b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "132" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444633} {:answer/id #uuid "6bca8e49-1df7-5904-85a2-f8689d1b6976" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "72" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444634} {:answer/id #uuid "6cdf6d31-4ecc-5d71-be94-0e1394489376" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "102" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444635} {:answer/id #uuid "b313ad8e-b7ae-5e7c-b6c7-5711116d766f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "124" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444638}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444638} :answer-field/id #uuid "e9075f10-8356-5116-9d8d-9363c1194e5f" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444649}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "e466b3e6-da95-5e08-8c31-060b08be1979" :question/math-academy-id "q-129524" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=3$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=1.$ Find $\\text{Var}[6X+4Y].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[6X+4Y]={6}^{2}\\cdot 3+{4}^{2}\\cdot 1=124.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "02103dc0-423e-557b-b063-22b229d61178" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444655}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444655 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444651 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0bbc9a21-53ce-5107-963d-75a23a6b47c8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "83" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444626} {:answer/id #uuid "1028e093-da26-54ef-aa43-a9f56a4e0493" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "57" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444627} {:answer/id #uuid "19ab9ab2-922f-5d8d-a24b-0e98ec213cd4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "130" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444628} {:answer/id #uuid "c9397ba9-a60b-592b-a489-b40297be8dd6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "4000" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444639} {:answer/id #uuid "d3eb23d5-5f09-5681-8d44-ab8a69f5b550" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "507" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444641}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444627} :answer-field/id #uuid "d483090b-1213-58fe-9189-e5ba9f5e3d4e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444647}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "4592eee9-4d66-541e-b103-0104f487a3d0" :question/math-academy-id "q-129525" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=100$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=200.$ Find $\\text{Var}[0.5X+0.4Y].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[0.5X+0.4Y]={0.5}^{2}\\cdot 100+{0.4}^{2}\\cdot 200=57.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "49059d24-c0ba-5351-8b41-92a57ba5b0cf" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444657}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444656 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444650 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2ede9d25-89b7-5e8f-9713-24a9987fd2b8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "21" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444630} {:answer/id #uuid "34df106a-01bf-52c7-8732-4b94b3d12e4e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "97" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444631} {:answer/id #uuid "d395ea19-98e4-51f7-840e-a5ea2d7b8782" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "95" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444640} {:answer/id #uuid "f52d6a13-0e5e-5a4a-a0e1-bd443eb90f6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "27" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444644} {:answer/id #uuid "f7e27671-31ae-5207-9120-d8a7d847ed6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444645}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444631} :answer-field/id #uuid "e537044c-a663-5a85-97de-b5e333853075" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444648}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485} :question/id #uuid "10f2f9d4-7d62-5e91-afc7-8a268909f88f" :question/math-academy-id "q-129540" :question/problem "Consider two independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ with expectations $\\text{E}[X]=8,$ $\\text{E}[Y]=11$\nand second raw moments $\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]=70$ and $\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]=130.$ Find the variance of  $4X-\\frac{1}{3}Y.$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of a random variable $X$ is determined by the formula \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X]:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =70-{8}^{2} \\\\  & =6.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =130-{11}^{2} \\\\  & =9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[4X-\\frac{1}{3}Y] & ={4}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{(-\\frac{1}{3})}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & =16\\cdot 6+\\frac{1}{9}\\cdot 9 \\\\  & =97.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "7a96e1af-ab36-58c9-a806-16a16fb24b77"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444657 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444652 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "1a5a011e-bc53-5bc4-8f45-39b4c9f6a5b6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "-15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444629} {:answer/id #uuid "97641fd2-bedb-53d0-a380-c9fdb8513014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "20" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444636} {:answer/id #uuid "9dd32b7a-4ad9-58cc-91f9-9026f1bd2828" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444637} {:answer/id #uuid "d66b4a53-dbb5-5db6-b6ce-d24df3369ea2" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "15" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444642} {:answer/id #uuid "ee19091b-a43a-5af6-af43-e22fe399a03b" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "1" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444643}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444637} :answer-field/id #uuid "4f087f72-105f-5680-aa6c-80acf118c1cb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444646}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "4ebe566e-595e-53c5-acba-b32f38c7b929" :question/math-academy-id "q-129526" :question/problem "For two independent random variables $X$ and $Y,$ we have $\\text{Var}[X]=20$ and $\\text{Var}[Y]=5.$ Find $\\text{Var}[0.5X-2Y].$" :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent variables, and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nSubstituting the given data into the formula above, we obtain \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[0.5X-2Y]={(0.5)}^{2}\\cdot 20+{(-2)}^{2}\\cdot 5=25.\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "9e6bf258-9f58-5ea9-aa72-d9183b6042e3" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444656}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444658 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186162277 :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "da3aff7b-959d-50a7-8381-c2a8dc46959a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "28.4" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158865} {:answer/id #uuid "e134b7b6-82d4-5373-8265-174fe3a4abfd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "6.24" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158866} {:answer/id #uuid "25535468-614f-5d32-a108-3120ed29894f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "35.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158867} {:answer/id #uuid "53529517-7235-5f48-9f2c-62ffe766939e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "3.25" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158868} {:answer/id #uuid "5c7a33d0-cb7f-52e9-b12c-edb84cc778dd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498} :answer/value "13.8" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158869}] :answer-field/correct {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186158866} :answer-field/id #uuid "e734c878-89fd-5f9c-b368-80d47341e5c1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186158864}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487} :question/id #uuid "50853964-7ea7-5098-a333-b9b8507d9ac8" :question/math-academy-id "q-109099" :question/problem "The probability mass function of the independent random variables $X$ and $Y$ are given below.\n\n<table align=\"center\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$x$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$3$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f(x)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.2$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.3$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.5$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n<th width=\"5%\"></th>\n<th>\n<table align=\"center\" cellpadding=\"10\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$y$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$1$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$2$</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" bgcolor=\"#D6EAF8\" width=\"30\">$f(y)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.25$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$0.75$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n<p></p></th>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>\n\n Find $\\text{Var}[3X+2Y].$" :question/requires-calculator true :question/worked-solution "Recall that if $X$ and $Y$ are independent random variables and $a$ and $b$ are constants, then\n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[aX+bY]={a}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{b}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y].\n$$\n\nAlso, the variance of $X$ is determined by the formula \n\n$$\n\\text{Var}[X]=\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2}.\n$$\n\nFirst, let's find $\\text{Var}[X].$ If $X$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{X}$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[X] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}x\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+2\\cdot 0.3+3\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =2.3\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{X}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{x\\in {S}_{X}}{x}^{2}\\cdot f(x) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.2+4\\cdot 0.3+9\\cdot 0.5 \\\\  & =5.9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[X] & =\\text{E}[{X}^{2}]-\\text{E}[X]^{2} \\\\  & =5.9-{2.3}^{2} \\\\  & =0.61.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSimilarly, if $Y$ is defined on a sample space ${S}_{Y}$, then\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[Y] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}y\\cdot f(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+2\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =1.75\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}] & ={\\sum }_{y\\in {S}_{Y}}{y}^{2}\\cdot f(y) \\\\  & =1\\cdot 0.25+4\\cdot 0.75 \\\\  & =3.25.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[Y] & =\\text{E}[{Y}^{2}]-\\text{E}[Y]^{2} \\\\  & =3.25-{1.75}^{2} \\\\  & =0.1875.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{Var}[3X-2Y] & ={3}^{2}\\text{Var}[X]+{2}^{2}\\text{Var}[Y] \\\\  & ={3}^{2}(0.61)+{2}^{2}(0.1875) \\\\  & =6.24.\\end{aligned}\n$$"} :step/id #uuid "ac8e29e0-f93f-5ae4-ae4f-10bb14033e1c" :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186444654}}] :activity/title "Variance of Sums of Independent Random Variables" :activity/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :activity.type/review} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186444625}]}
